course: adult-life
lesson: 62
title: Olympiad: plans in order and money sums ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This last olympiad lesson mixes the grown-up plans and the money of the whole course into puzzles: what must come first, what is left after the bills, and which deal is really cheaper.
explain: A grown-up plan is a chain: you cannot book a van for moving day before you know where you are moving, and you cannot send wedding invitations before the date and the place are fixed.
explain: Money puzzles need only adding, taking away, times and sharing, but they hide traps: a sign that says «half price» when it is not, a «free» item you never needed, a loan that costs more than the price.
explain: Scene: in a flat in Bristol, Dad has £100 for the week. He writes on the back of an envelope: food £45, bus fares £20, power £15. «Right, that leaves £20 for everything else,» he says, and circles it.
explain: For every puzzle ask: What has to happen first? Which numbers really count? What is the total at the end, and is there a cheaper way?
fact: In a plan some steps must come first: the date and the place before the invitations, the new home before the van, the van before moving day.
fact: Renting a new home goes in order: find the home, sign and pay the deposit, book a van, pack, move in, then give people the new address.
fact: A budget starts with the money coming in, takes away the bills, and what is left is for extras and saving.
fact: To find how many weeks of saving are needed, take away what you already have from the price, then share the rest by the amount saved each week.
fact: Borrowing costs extra: if you borrow £40 and pay back £46, the extra £6 is interest, while saving up first costs nothing extra.
fact: Half price means the new price is half the old one: half of £24 is £12, so a sign saying «was £24, now £16, half price» is wrong.
fact: «Buy 2, get 1 free» saves money only if you really need all three.
fact: A family ticket is only a good deal after checking: add up the single tickets for your family and compare.
fact: Change is the money handed over take away the price.
fact: Buy now pay later splits a price into equal payments; the total is still the full price, and a late payment adds a charge.
fact: A month is a bit more than 4 weeks, so £5 a week costs more than £20 a month.
fact: When money comes in, the must-pay bills (rent, power, food) come before treats.
fact: If a bank card is lost, the bank should be told at once so it can block the card.
fact: Hyperbole numbers («cost an arm and a leg», «a million quid») are never counted, and after «no wait» the last number said is the one that counts.
exercise: Which order makes sense for renting a new home? | Find a home, pay the deposit, book the van, then move in | Book the van, pack, find a home, pay the deposit | Pack, move in, find a home, book the van | Pay the deposit, find a home, move in, book the van
exercise: For a wedding, which step must come before the invitations go out? | Fixing the date and the place | Cutting the wedding cake into slices | Picking the song for the first dance | Writing all the thank-you cards
exercise: Mia wants a £30 game. She has £6 and saves £4 a week. How many weeks until she can buy it? | 6 | 7 | 8 | 5
exercise: Dad has £100 for the week: food £45, bus fares £20, power £15. How much is left? | £20 | £30 | £25 | £35
exercise: Auntie borrows £40 and pays back £46. How much interest did she pay? | £6 | £46 | £4 | £86
exercise: A sign says «HALF PRICE! Was £24, now £16.» Is it right? | No, half of £24 would be £12 | Yes, £16 is half of £24 | No, half of £24 is £14 | Yes, as the sign says so
exercise: At the zoo: adult £10, child £5, family ticket (2 adults and 2 children) £25. How much does a family of 2 adults and 2 children save with it? | £5 | £10 | £0 | £15
exercise: Same zoo prices. What is the cheapest way in for 2 adults and 3 children? | A family ticket and one child ticket, £30 | Five single tickets, £35 in all | Two family tickets, £50 in all | Just the family ticket, £25
exercise: You buy a comic for £3.40 and a drink for £2.80 and pay with £10. How much change? | £3.80 | £4.80 | £3.20 | £6.20
exercise: The same app costs £5 a week or £18 a month. Which is cheaper? | £18 a month, as a month is over 4 weeks | £5 a week, as 5 is the smaller number | They cost exactly the same in the end | £5 a week, because weeks are shorter
exercise: Trainers cost £60 on buy now pay later, in 3 equal payments. How much is each payment? | £20 | £30 | £15 | £60
exercise: Pay arrives on the 1st. In which order should the money go? | Rent and power, then food, then treats | Treats first, while there is still money | Food, then treats, then rent if any is left | Saving first, then treats, then the rent
exercise: Mum works nights from 10 pm to 6 am. How long is her shift? | 8 hours | 16 hours | 12 hours | 10 hours
exercise: Shampoo is £3. Offer: «Buy 2, get 1 free.» You only need one bottle. Is the offer good for you? | No, you pay £6 for what £3 would buy | Yes, because one bottle is free | Yes, it saves you £3 in the end | No, the free bottle costs £3 extra
exercise: A bike is £90. Plan A: save £10 a week for 9 weeks. Plan B: borrow and pay £11 a week for 9 weeks. How much more does Plan B cost? | £9 | £11 | £99 | £1
exercise: Mum can't find her bank card anywhere. What should she do first? | Tell the bank so it can block the card | Wait a week to see if it turns up | Order herself a new purse online | Ask the neighbours if they took it
test: Kai from Peckham: «Pocket money's £6 — nah wait, £5 this week, bruv. Spent £2 on sweets.» How much has he got left? | £3 | £4 | £1 | £5
test: Uncle: «That pizza place cost an arm and a leg — £18 for two pizzas!» How much was each pizza? | £9 | £18 | £36 | £6
test: Isla from Dundee: «Aye, three bus tickets at £2.50 each, and I've a £10 note, wee man.» What change does she get? | £2.50 | £7.50 | £3.50 | £1.50
test: «Got them trainers on pay later, £60 in three goes. Missed one, they slapped on a £5 fee. Gutted.» What do they cost in total now? | £65 | £60 | £55 | £80
test: Gran's operation day, mixed up: A go home, B the operation, C check in at the hospital, D rest on the ward. What's the right order? | C, B, D, A | B, C, D, A | C, D, B, A | A, C, B, D
test: Four mates share a £22 pizza bill evenly. «Lowkey, just call it a fiver each,» says one. How much should each really pay? | £5.50 | £5 | £6 | £4.40
test: Auntie bought 5 jumpers at £8 each «cos they were practically giving them away», and never wore one. How much went on things she didn't need? | £40 | £8 | £13 | Nothing at all, it was a sale
test: Dad: «Let's tell school our new address now, we'll find a house after.» What's wrong with the plan? | There's no new address to give until a home is found | Schools never need to know a new address | The school must find the family a house first | Nothing, telling school first is the right order
test: «Saving £3 a week, sorry, £4 a week, for a £20 speaker, sound.» How many weeks does it take? | 5 | 7 | 6 | 4
test: Phone deal: «£8 a month for 12 months, or £90 paid now for the whole year. Which is cheaper, chur?» | Paying the £90 now saves £6 | Paying monthly saves £6 | They cost the same, £90 | Paying monthly saves £2
test: «Shut up and count it — Nan gave me £20, Grandad £15, and I spent £12 on a game.» How much is left? | £23 | £35 | £47 | £13
exam: Determine the interest payable upon a loan of £50 repaid in full as £58. | £8 | £58 | £50 | £108
exam: Select the correct sequence of steps in renting a new home. | Find a home, pay the deposit, book a van, move in | Book a van, move in, find a home, pay the deposit | Pay the deposit, book a van, find a home, move in | Move in, find a home, book a van, pay the deposit
exam: Identify the more economical arrangement: £5 payable weekly or £19 payable monthly. | £19 monthly, as a month exceeds four weeks | £5 weekly, being the smaller sum on each occasion | Neither, the two being exactly equal in cost | £5 weekly, as weeks are fewer in number overall
exam: Ascertain the number of weeks required to save for an item costing £36, given £8 already held and £4 saved weekly. | 7 | 9 | 8 | 10
